/**
 * @mainpage AdGIA
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 * An open-source finite-element library, built on
 * <a href="https://mfem.org">MFEM</a>, for modelling glacial isostatic
 * adjustment and related processes such as post-seismic deformation, with
 * the forward problem and its first- and second-order adjoints. The
 * solid-Earth part of the forward problem is in place: quasi-static
 * deformation of a self-gravitating elastic or viscoelastic body, with fluid
 * regions, general (non-hydrostatic, non-natural) reference states and
 * slipping fluid–solid interfaces. The sea-level equation, rotational
 * feedbacks, shoreline migration and the first-order adjoints are in place;
 * the interface kernels and the second-order adjoints are still to come.
 * Serial and parallel (MPI) paths are provided throughout, normally in one
 * class.
 *
 * @section contents Library contents
 *
 * @subsection sec_index Component ordering
 * `index.hpp` fixes the component ordering and the node/component indexing
 * of vector, matrix, symmetric and trace-free symmetric tensor fields
 * (SymmetricComponentOrder, VectorIndex, MatrixIndex, SymmetricMatrixIndex,
 * TraceFreeSymmetricMatrixIndex). Every integrator, interpolator and
 * coefficient below follows it.
 *
 * @subsection sec_integrators Integrators, interpolators and coefficients
 * `bilininteg.hpp` and `lininteg.hpp` hold:
 * - mixed integrators between vector, scalar and tensor nodal spaces (the
 *   non-standard terms of the self-gravitating weak form and the
 *   internal-variable couplings of viscoelasticity) and the strain
 *   interpolators;
 * - ElasticTensorIntegrator for general anisotropic elasticity;
 * - the initial-stress (total-Lagrangian) stiffness split
 *   MaterialStiffnessIntegrator / GeometricStiffnessIntegrator, and the
 *   referential gravity terms ReferentialGravityIntegrator /
 *   ReferentialGravityCouplingIntegrator;
 * - the boundary integrators of the fluid–solid interface terms
 *   (BoundaryNormalNormalIntegrator, BoundaryNormalScalarIntegrator) and of
 *   slipping interfaces (SlipInterfacePressureIntegrator,
 *   SlipInterfaceGravityIntegrator, SlipInterfaceGravityScalarIntegrator);
 * - TransformedDiffusionIntegrator for Poisson problems posed on a
 *   reference domain.
 *
 * Most of them have constructors taking a Diffeomorphism and then assemble
 * the form pulled back through it (see `mappings.hpp` below).
 * `coefficient.hpp` holds general-purpose coefficients, and
 * `elastic_tensor.hpp` the elastic tensor coefficients in the Mandel
 * convention of SymmetricTensorBasis: isotropic, transversely isotropic,
 * Voigt, rotated, deviatoric projection, the relabelling transformation
 * (RelabelledElasticTensorCoefficient) and the conversion of seismological
 * (PREM) moduli to the bare tensor (BareElasticTensorCoefficient). The
 * mathematical formulation of each is in its class documentation.
 *
 * @subsection sec_poisson Exterior Poisson problems and harmonics
 * `poisson.hpp` provides the matrix-free Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator on a
 * spherical outer boundary (PoissonDtNOperator) and the multipole
 * operators (PoissonMultipoleOperator, PoissonLinearisedMultipoleOperator),
 * built on the real orthonormal harmonics of `spherical_harmonics.hpp`
 * (SurfaceHarmonics), which also synthesises fields from coefficients
 * (HarmonicExpansionCoefficient) and analyses finite-element fields on
 * spherical boundaries (BoundaryHarmonicCoefficients). `mesh.hpp` has the
 * mesh queries: attribute markers, centroids, spherical-boundary detection.
 *
 * @subsection sec_submesh Coupling a mesh and its SubMesh
 * `submesh.hpp`: SubMeshDofInjection, the signed dof injection of a space on
 * a SubMesh into the corresponding space on the parent mesh; the dof
 * pairing of two sibling SubMeshes (NewSubMeshPairingMatrix); and
 * SubMeshMixedBilinearForm / ParSubMeshMixedBilinearForm, mixed forms whose
 * two spaces live on a mesh and one of its SubMeshes.
 *
 * @subsection sec_problems Quasi-static problems and rheologies
 * LinearQuasiStaticProblem (`quasi_static_problem.hpp`) is the interface of
 * a linear quasi-static equilibrium problem; LinearQuasiStaticProblemBase
 * implements it on a serial or parallel displacement space, including the
 * treatment of fluid regions by SetFluid (Maxwell relaxation by default, the
 * gauge penalty with GaugePenaltyOptions), with
 * LinearQuasiStaticTractionProblem and LinearQuasiStaticClampedProblem as
 * reference problems. The material is a Rheology (`rheology.hpp`): elastic
 * or generalised Maxwell, isotropic or anisotropic, optionally with
 * state-dependent relaxation times (RelaxationLaw, `relaxation_law.hpp`),
 * and different in different regions of the body (CompositeRheology).
 *
 * @subsection sec_selfgrav Self-gravitation
 * - LinearQuasiStaticMixedSelfGravitatingProblem (`mixed_problem.hpp`)
 *   couples the displacement on a SubMesh of the body to the Eulerian
 *   potential perturbation on the enclosing ball, with the DtN outer
 *   condition. Fluid regions are treated either in Dahlen's way
 *   (FluidRegion) or in the displacement space through SetFluid: by Maxwell
 *   relaxation, the general method, or by the gauge penalty, clean for
 *   neutrally stratified fluids. It requires a hydrostatic, natural
 *   reference state.
 * - LinearQuasiStaticReferentialSelfGravitatingProblem
 *   (`referential_problem.hpp`) is the fully referential formulation about
 *   a general reference state (bare elastic tensor, equilibrium stress and
 *   equilibrium mapping, ReferentialElasticRheology), with a prescribed
 *   vacuum extension of the displacement into the buffer.
 * - LinearQuasiStaticReferentialSelfGravitatingSlipProblem adds a slipping
 *   fluid–solid interface: a broken displacement pair constrained by
 *   normal continuity, enforced by penalty with augmented-Lagrangian
 *   iterations or by a KKT system, with a single-valued or broken
 *   referential potential.
 *
 * All three implement LinearQuasiStaticProblem, so the viscoelastic layer
 * runs on them unchanged. For fluid regions the Maxwell relaxation of
 * SetFluid is the general method (any geometry, stratification and number of
 * regions); the gauge penalty is clean only for neutrally stratified fluids
 * (at N² ≠ 0 its semi-convergence is Rayleigh–Taylor growth); Dahlen's mixed
 * formulation is the workhorse for hydrostatic models, and the slipping
 * and welded formulations are idealisations that serve as instruments
 * (`doc/static_fluid_core.tex`).
 *
 * @subsection sec_background Background states
 * `background.hpp` generates the reference state the referential classes
 * consume: the hydrostatic state of a radial model (RadialHydrostaticState,
 * RadialHydrostaticBackground), the same state seen from a relabelled
 * reference body (RelabelledBackground), the equilibrium stress fields of
 * minimum norm and minimum deviatoric norm for aspherical bodies
 * (MinimumNormEquilibriumStress, MinimumDeviatoricEquilibriumStress), and
 * the harmonic extension of a body mapping into the buffer
 * (NewHarmonicExtensionMapping).
 *
 * @subsection sec_mappings Mappings
 * `mappings.hpp` is the relabelling layer: Diffeomorphism and its concrete
 * classes (IdentityDiffeomorphism, CallableDiffeomorphism,
 * RadialDiffeomorphism, TaperedDiffeomorphism, GridFunctionDiffeomorphism),
 * the free functions Interpolate, MappedMesh and MaxIdentityDeviation, and
 * the pull-back and Nanson coefficients (JacobianCoefficient,
 * PullbackDiffusionCoefficient, PullbackGradientCoefficient,
 * PullbackStressCoefficient, NansonAreaCoefficient,
 * MappedBoundaryNormalDotCoefficient, the Transformed*FunctionCoefficient
 * family).
 *
 * @subsection sec_visco Viscoelasticity
 * ViscoelasticOperator (`viscoelastic.hpp`) evolves the internal variables
 * of a generalised Maxwell body around any LinearQuasiStaticProblem, with
 * explicit, implicit and exponential time stepping (ExponentialEulerSolver,
 * ExponentialTrapezoidSolver, AdaptiveExponentialTrapezoidSolver). The
 * referential classes are elastic-only apart from the artificial Maxwell
 * relaxation of their fluid regions, which is a solution device and not a
 * physical rheology.
 *
 * @subsection sec_sealevel Sea level, rotation and shoreline migration
 * `sea_level.hpp`, `rotation.hpp` and `centrifugal.hpp`: the water-load
 * feedback of the sea-level equation and its uniform-term and
 * angular-velocity borders on the mixed and the referential problems
 * (SetWaterLoad, SetRotation, with elimination and monolithic solve routes;
 * the rotation through the traditional border or the generalised Tisserand
 * door), shoreline migration (ShorelineMigration), the surface layer
 * (SeaLevelOperator) and ice-history loading (IceHistory). `bordered.hpp`
 * (BorderedElimination) eliminates the appended scalar unknowns.
 * RefineToShoreline (`mesh.hpp`) refines a mesh along the shoreline.
 *
 * @subsection sec_adjoint Adjoints
 * `adjoint.hpp`: the first-order adjoint force (AdjointForce), the forward
 * trajectory store (ForwardTrajectory), kernel accumulators
 * (KernelAccumulator, IsotropicKernel, SeaLevelLoadKernel) and the adjoint
 * driver (AdjointSolver) for static and viscoelastic, gravitating problems,
 * including the water-load and rotation feedbacks; kernels for viscosity,
 * ice load and moduli.
 *
 * @subsection sec_descent Riesz maps and descent
 * `riesz.hpp`: Riesz maps (L2RieszMap, SobolevRieszMap) identifying
 * derivative duals with gradients in L2 and in (iterated, vector-valued)
 * Sobolev metrics. `descent.hpp`: the constrained metric
 * (ConstrainedMetric) and the projected nonlinear-CG and Levenberg–Marquardt
 * Gauss–Newton loops over them.
 *
 * @subsection sec_figures Equilibrium figures
 * `equilibrium_figures.hpp`: the density feasibility functional
 * (DensityFeasibility, DensityFeasibilityProblem) of the equilibrium-figures
 * programme and its adjoint-free derivative. The shape stage is not yet
 * implemented.
 *
 * @subsection sec_null_space Null-space projection
 * NullSpaceProjector (`null_space.hpp`) holds an orthonormal basis of a
 * (near-)null space, and ProjectedSolver / ProjectedOperator run a Krylov
 * method on the projected system @f$P A P@f$ with the projected
 * preconditioner @f$P M P@f$. MakeRigidModeProjector() builds the projector
 * for the rigid modes of a displacement space (RigidTranslation,
 * RigidRotation, and MappedRotation for a mapped reference body); this is
 * how problems with traction boundary conditions are solved (see
 * `examples/quasi_static_elasticity.cpp`).
 *
 * @subsection sec_manifest Mesh manifests and model files
 * planetmodel's C++ library reads the JSON manifest that planetmodel
 * writes beside a mesh (`planetmodel::MeshManifest`); the glue in
 * `manifest.hpp` opens the mesh and the model fields it names and turns
 * its attribute lists into the markers the problems take. A netCDF
 * model file exported beside the mesh becomes MFEM coefficients through
 * `model_coefficient.hpp`: planetmodel's interpolants evaluated at the
 * integration points, the layer the element's attribute.
 *
 * @section docs Documentation index
 * Paths are relative to the source tree.
 *
 * Reference (how the library works and why):
 * - `doc/quasi_static_models.tex` (PDF beside it): every problem class —
 *   model, assumptions, discretisation, solvers, verification.
 * - `doc/gravitating_elasticity.md`: the linearised theory of
 *   self-gravitating, pre-stressed elasticity behind the referential
 *   classes and the background module.
 * - `doc/self_gravitation.md`: the mixed problem with Dahlen fluid regions,
 *   its fluid–solid interface conditions and CMB approximations.
 * - `doc/gauged_fluid.md`: the gauge-penalty treatment of fluid regions.
 * - `doc/static_fluid_core.tex`: the static fluid-core problem and the status
 *   of the formulations (Maxwell relaxation, gauge penalty, Dahlen, slipping,
 *   welded).
 * - `doc/slip_interface.tex`: the slipping fluid–solid interface —
 *   derivation, discretisation, constraint enforcement, verification.
 * - `doc/gauge_penalty_iteration.tex`: gauge penalties and their iterated
 *   refinement.
 * - `doc/generalised_rotation.md`: the generalised (Maitra & Al-Attar)
 *   rotational theory — the traditional border's gauge ambiguity, the
 *   Tisserand resolution and its secular reduction.
 * - `doc/mappings.md`: the mapping (relabelling) layer.
 * - `doc/submesh_coupling.md`, `doc/viscoelasticity.md`,
 *   `doc/elastic_tensors.md`, `doc/null_space.md`, `doc/mfem_notes.md`:
 *   SubMesh coupling, rheologies and time stepping, elastic tensors,
 *   projected solvers, MFEM facts and pitfalls.
 * - `doc/equilibrium_figures.tex`: equilibrium states and hydrostatic
 *   figures by constrained optimisation.
 * - `doc/benchmarks.tex`: the benchmark families and their results.
 * - `doc/BenchmarkPapers/code_survey.md`: a survey of the published 3-D
 *   GIA codes and benchmarks, set against the library's treatments.
 * - `INSTALL.md`, `examples/README.md`, `meshes/README.md`,
 *   `benchmarks/README.md`, `earth_data/README.md`.
 *
 * Planning (open issues and future work, not a description of the code):
 * `doc/planning/`, indexed by its `README.md` — `open_issues.md`,
 * `solvers.md`, `future_work.md`, `equilibrium_figures.md`.
 *
 * @section install Installation
 * MFEM must be built first, serial or parallel. Configuration is with CMake;
 * in-source builds are refused. `INSTALL.md` lists the prerequisites and
 * every build option (`USE_MPI`, `BUILD_EXAMPLES`, `BUILD_TESTS`,
 * `BUILD_BENCHMARKS`, `BUILD_DOCS`, mesh generation) with its default.
 *
 * @section proj_structure Project structure
 * - `include/`, `src/`: the library (`include/AdGIA.hpp` includes
 *   every public header).
 * - `examples/`: example programs (`BUILD_EXAMPLES`).
 * - `tests/`: googletest suite and MPI test programs (`BUILD_TESTS`).
 * - `benchmarks/`: comparisons with independent reference solutions
 *   (`BUILD_BENCHMARKS`, MPI builds only).
 * - `meshes/`: Python scripts generating the gmsh meshes into the build
 *   tree's `data/`.
 * - `data/`: small MFEM-format meshes copied to the build tree.
 * - `doc/`: Doxygen configuration, this page, and the documents listed
 *   above.
 */
